<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Multiplication law for independent events</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>[[if test="is(cx=0)"]]A tea factory in Kericho has two machines, A and B. In a given month, the probability that machine A needs a repair is \({@pdec(p)@}\) and the probability that machine B needs a repair is \({@pdec(q)@}\).[[elif test="is(cx=1)"]]A shop sells solar lamps from two suppliers, A and B. The probability that a lamp from supplier A fails in its first year is \({@pdec(p)@}\), and for a lamp from supplier B it is \({@pdec(q)@}\). Kibet buys one lamp from each supplier.[[else]]Kamau plants a grevillea seedling in Kitui and Akinyi plants a mango seedling in Kisii. The probability that Kamau's seedling survives its first dry season is \({@pdec(p)@}\) and the probability that Akinyi's seedling survives is \({@pdec(q)@}\).[[/if]] These events are independent.</p><p>Give each probability exactly, as a fraction or an exact decimal.</p><p>(a) Find the probability that [[if test="is(cx=0)"]]both machines need a repair[[elif test="is(cx=1)"]]both lamps fail[[else]]both seedlings survive[[/if]].</p><div>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div><p>(b) Find the probability that [[if test="is(cx=0)"]]neither machine needs a repair[[elif test="is(cx=1)"]]neither lamp fails[[else]]neither seedling survives[[/if]].</p><div>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div><p>(c) Find the probability that [[if test="is(cx=0)"]]machine A needs a repair and machine B does not[[elif test="is(cx=1)"]]the lamp from A fails and the lamp from B does not[[else]]Kamau's seedling survives and Akinyi's does not[[/if]].</p><div>[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>The events are independent, so for <q>and</q> multiply the probabilities.</p><p>(a) \({@pdec(p)@} \times {@pdec(q)@} = {@pdec(ta1)@}\).</p><p>(b) The probabilities that they do not happen are \(1 - {@pdec(p)@} = {@pdec(1-p)@}\) and \(1 - {@pdec(q)@} = {@pdec(1-q)@}\). Neither: \({@pdec(1-p)@} \times {@pdec(1-q)@} = {@pdec(ta2)@}\).</p><p>(c) The first happens and the second does not: \({@pdec(p)@} \times {@pdec(1-q)@} = {@pdec(ta3)@}\).</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>3</defaultgrade>
  <penalty>0.1</penalty>
